60 years ago today communist east  germany began the construction of the  berlin wall overnight families and  friends were separated and the lives and  destinies of countless people were  changed forever by the time it fell in  1989 more than 100 people had been  killed trying to get over or under the  wall to freedom in the west east  germany's leaders said it was an  anti-fascist barrier but the wall became  a symbol of repression preventing east  germans from leaving  from the very beginning many of them  tried to do just that dw's tessa valta  spoke to an eyewitness of one of the  first and most famous examples of  jumping the wall  was still a young man when he witnessed  this historic moment  the east german border policeman conrad  schumann jumped over the border fence  between east and west berlin  today the wall is gone here only iron  bars mark the former border but the 82  year old still remembers it clearly yes  [Music]  this is exactly where i was standing a  few people were in front of me  and we just saw him as he was jumping  over the fence  we all cheered and we welcomed him with  open arms  it was right here  [Music]  in the years before more and more people  had fled from east to west berlin  escaping the communist dictatorship  in 1961  east germany deployed border guards to  stop the population drain one of them  was conrad schumann  but instead of defending communism he  chose to jump to the democratic west  it took a lot of courage to dare jumping  over that fence especially as a border  guard that was really great if i had  been on duty with all the other guards  around i don't think i would have had  the courage i mean they could have shot  [Music]  what began with a bit of barbed wire  soon became a solid concrete structure  the berlin wall  it divided the city for 28 years  it tore families and friendships apart  [Music]  now 60 years after the war was built ben  bruder has a clear message for the  younger generations who never  experienced it  we need to fight to make sure that a war  is never again built here in germany let  alone a national border  and we should all make sure that there  is never another war in germany  germany's and berlin's reunification is  still today one of the greatest  occasions in his country's history  our chief political correspondent  melinda crane is at the berlin wall  memorial for us good morning melinda how  is this day being commemorated  that message that we just heard at the  end of the report a message of how  important it is to remember and how  important it is that walls never again  uh be built that's exactly the message  we're hearing right here right now at a  commemoration  ceremony i'm standing at the bernauer  straza wall memorial this is the place  where conrad schumann jumped the wall as  it was being built and it's also a place  where the east german communist regime  dynamited a church to create a  defensible zone along the wall you can  see it behind me  the so-called death strip president  frank walter steinmeier is speaking  right now we will also hear from  berlin's lord mayor michelle miller with  similar messages uh calling for  remembrance and the need to defend and  be committed to freedom and to  historical witnesses who saw the events  here 60 years ago we'll also be speaking  well  germany is holding elections next month  30 years after reunification how are  german politics still shaped by that  history itself  many people  say that in fact we have two electorates  in germany one in the east and one in  the west the far-right afd party with  its populist anti-immigrant messages  regularly wins a significantly larger  share of the vote in the eastern states  uh up  20  and more and is the second strongest  political party in many of those states  the fact is that has more to do with  what happened after the fall of the wall  than before many east germans who fought  for freedoms in 1989 had the feeling  that afterward they became second-class  citizens they saw companies shut down  many young people move away to better  jobs in the west uh and wages today are  still lower in the east than the than  the west unemployment is higher in many  parts of the east for all those reasons  many east germans are receptive to the  identity politics of the afd far-right  party  and melinda if we talk about the wall  itself a lot of people thought think of  it as having been built suddenly but  that wasn't the case and historians are  finding out more about that what can you  tell us  no in fact on june 15th 1961  the head of the east german communist  party central committee while walter  ulbricht told the press no one has the  intention of building a wall what we  know now is that as early as 1953 he had  appealed to russian leader nikita  khrushchev to close the border between  east berlin and west berlin and that the  east german regime had very seriously  considered putting up a barrier as early  as 1958 in order to stop a massive  exodus of east germans to the west  including people of working age who were  badly needed another fact that has  emerged fairly recently the east german  regime was prepared for massive protests  on that august 13 60 years ago they took  the necessary precautions and then in  fact the eastern party of the city was  relatively quiet and it seems that many  east berliners  accepted or perhaps even approved of the  construction of the wall thinking it was  the only way to preserve the economic  viability of a communist dream that many  of them still believed in and the fact  is some were also resentful of east  berliners who had been computing to the  west enjoying privileges there higher  salaries consumer goods and of course  the construction of the wall did put an  end to that  our chief political correspondent  melinda green craned for us at the  berlin wall memorial today thank you
 

